\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

A recommended 3-prompt $\times$ 3-seed design reduces item requirements from 552 to 178 for $G \geq 0.80$ (full multi-facet: 55 items), confirmed across all five benchmarks (bootstrap CIs exceed 10$\times$; budgets validated for 7--9B models under binary scoring). We observe suggestive but underpowered format-associated differences warranting larger benchmark samples. A minimal upgrade---two prompt variants---yields the steepest per-condition reliability gain (Appendix~\ref{app:intermediate_dstudy}); code, data, and a D-study calculator will be released upon publication.

\section{Experiments}

All experiments use open-weight 7--9B parameter models evaluated with vLLM~0.9.2 on a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPU (96GB) with binary correctness scoring.

\subsection{Single-Model Baseline}

We first establish the variance structure within a single model (Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct) on MMLU to isolate methodological facet contributions before introducing cross-model variation.

\paragraph{Design.} The G-study crosses six facets: 200 items $\times$ 3 numerical precisions (BF16, FP16, FP32) $\times$ 6 prompt templates $\times$ 6 random seeds $\times$ 3 temperature levels (0.0, 0.3, 0.7) $\times$ 4 answer orderings, yielding 259,200 binary observations. Variance components are estimated via Henderson Method~I with bootstrap confidence intervals (1,000 resamples).

\paragraph{Results.} Under binary scoring, item difficulty (\texttt{item\_id}) accounts for 58.4\% of total variance, with the residual term contributing 23.1\%. All main facet effects are negligible ($<$1\% each): precision 0.9\%, prompt template, temperature, seed, and ordering each $<$0.5\%. The interesting structure appears in item-conditional interactions: prompt$\times$item is the largest at 5.5\%, followed by seed$\times$item (4.7\%), precision$\times$item (3.3\%), temperature$\times$item (2.9\%), and ordering$\times$item (0.8\%). This pattern indicates that methodological choices do not shift performance uniformly across items; instead, specific items are differentially sensitive to specific facets---prompt template variation produces the largest item-conditional effect.

The resulting generalizability coefficient is $G_\text{item} = 0.936$, indicating that 200 items under multi-facet conditions produce highly reliable rankings. A D-study projection shows $G \geq 0.80$ is achievable with as few as 55 items in this multi-facet design.

For text-exact-match scoring (requiring verbatim string matches), the variance structure shifts dramatically: temperature dominates at 79.7\% of variance, and $G_\text{item}$ collapses to 0.007. This metric-conditional divergence motivates our exclusive use of binary correctness for the remaining analyses.

\subsection{Cross-Model Variance}

Extending to eight models (BF16 subset, 460,800 records), Table~\ref{tab:cross_model} shows that item difficulty drops from 58.4\% to 37.9\%, while model$\times$item interaction emerges at up to 36\%. The model main effect is 3--5\% (MC) and 9--11\% (FF), confirming that models differ primarily in \emph{which items} they find difficult rather than in overall ability level.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Variance decomposition for the cross-model MMLU design (8 models, BF16, 460,800 records). Components estimated via Henderson Method~I. $G_\text{item} = 0.896$.}
\label{tab:cross_model}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lr}
\toprule
Component & Var.\% \\
\midrule
\textbf{item\_id} & \textbf{37.9} \\
\underline{model$\times$item} & \underline{30.2} \\
residual & 25.0 \\
model & 2.8 \\
prompt$\times$item & 2.5 \\
model$\times$prompt & 1.6 \\
all others & $<$0.1 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Exploratory: Format-Associated Variance Patterns}
\label{sec:format_conditional}

We extend to five benchmarks (three MC: MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag; two FF: GSM8K, MATH) using exp-002 (8 models $\times$ BF16 $\times$ 2 temperatures $\times$ 6 prompts $\times$ 6 seeds $\times$ 4 orderings $\times$ 200 items). Table~\ref{tab:cross_benchmark} reveals a format-associated pattern. For all three MC benchmarks, item-related variance (item + model$\times$item DIF; e.g., MMLU: 37.9\% + 30.2\% = 68.1\%) concentrates at 68--72\%. HellaSwag is the sole MC case where model$\times$item slightly exceeds item (36.0\% vs.\ 34.3\%), though their sum remains in the MC range.

Free-form benchmarks differ markedly. On GSM8K, variance distributes across item (16.9\%), model$\times$item (14.6\%), model (10.7\%), and model$\times$prompt (8.5\%); prompt main effect is 5.4\%. MATH shows an intermediate pattern: item leads at 24.0\% with model$\times$item at 17.3\%, but residual variance is substantially larger (43.9\%). Cross-benchmark LOMO analysis shows that removing any single model changes $G_\text{item}$ by at most 0.030 across all five benchmarks (Appendix~\ref{app:robustness}). The elevated FF residuals (GSM8K 39.9\%, MATH 43.9\%) likely reflect sources not captured by our facets: chain-of-thought path diversity, generation stochasticity, and answer extraction heuristics; finer-grained scoring (e.g., partial credit) may reclassify portions into interpretable components. Since scoring granularity fundamentally reshapes variance structure (binary $G = 0.936$ vs.\ text-exact-match $G = 0.007$; \S4.1), our format-associated findings are conditional on binary scoring.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Format-associated variance structure across five benchmarks (exp-002, 8 models). Variance components (\%) per benchmark; MC = multiple-choice, FF = free-form. Bold = dominant non-residual component. ``Prompt'' row: for MC, reports prompt$\times$item interaction (prompt main effect $<$1\%); for FF, reports the prompt main effect (item-conditional interactions absorbed into residual due to higher generation variability).}
\label{tab:cross_benchmark}
\scriptsize
\begin{tabular}{l rrr rr}
\toprule
 & \multicolumn{3}{c}{MC} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{FF} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-6}
 & MMLU & ARC & Hella. & GSM & MATH \\
\midrule
\textbf{item} & \textbf{37.9} & \textbf{37.9} & 34.3 & 16.9 & 24.0 \\
mod.$\times$item & 30.2 & 33.7 & \textbf{36.0} & 14.6 & 17.3 \\
model & 2.8 & 3.1 & 5.4 & 10.7 & 8.5 \\
prompt & 2.5 & 0.4 & 2.3 & 5.4 & 1.0 \\
mod.$\times$prmpt & 1.6 & 8.0 & 0.6 & 8.5 & 2.0 \\
residual & 25.0 & 15.4 & 21.4 & 39.9 & 43.9 \\
all others & $<$1.0 & 1.5 & $<$1.0 & 4.0 & 3.3 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The following pattern is exploratory ($n = 5$ benchmarks; permutation resolution floor $p = 0.10$). MC benchmarks concentrate item-related variance at 68--72\%, while FF benchmarks disperse it to 31--41\%, with no overlap. Unbundled, item difficulty alone accounts for 34--38\% (MC) versus 17--24\% (FF), and model$\times$item DIF for 30--36\% (MC) versus 15--17\% (FF), maintaining complete separation in both components (unbundled benchmark-level $d = 4.84$, bootstrap 95\% CI $[2.32, 8.19]$), confirming that the format gap is not an artifact of DIF bundling. The exhaustive permutation test yields $p = 0.10$, the resolution floor for $\binom{5}{3} = 10$ partitions. The item-level effect size is strong ($d = 1.73$; 95\% CI $[1.72, 1.83]$), but the benchmark-level CI spans zero ($[-1.7, 5.1]$, $df = 3$); we therefore report this as a descriptive observation with strong item-level support. This does not extend to item budgets: GSM8K requires 128 items for $G \geq 0.80$ while HellaSwag (MC, 118) exceeds both ARC (98) and MATH (101), showing that format alone does not predict evaluation cost. Figure~\ref{fig:cross_benchmark} visualizes the contrast.

The factorial design is not fully symmetric: ordering applies only to MC (FF items lack permutable options), and prompt templates serve format-specific functions (option layout for MC, CoT elicitation for FF), potentially confounding format comparisons.

One might argue the format gap is tautological: MC constrains generation noise, mechanically inflating item-related shares. Two observations complicate this. First, HellaSwag is MC yet model$\times$item (36.0\%) exceeds item (34.3\%)---pure format effects would produce uniform item dominance. Second, cross-benchmark difficulty dispersion ($\text{std}(p_i)$) shows a strong positive association with item variance share ($\rho = 0.82$, $n = 5$; Appendix~\ref{app:difficulty_scatter}), and within MMLU, \texttt{high\_school\_mathematics} shows item variance of just 10.4\%, below GSM8K (16.9\%). Format constrains the noise floor, but item difficulty heterogeneity determines the magnitude of item-related variance within that floor.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig1_cross_benchmark}
\caption{Variance decomposition (Henderson Method~I) across five benchmarks and eight models. Multiple-choice benchmarks share an item-dominated structure; HellaSwag is the exception where model$\times$item (36.0\%) exceeds item difficulty (34.3\%). Free-form benchmarks (GSM8K, MATH) distribute variance across item, model, and interaction components with no single dominant source.}
\label{fig:cross_benchmark}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Precision Separability}

Under binary scoring, BF16 and FP16 are functionally interchangeable ($<$0.1\% interaction with all other facets \cite{yuan2025layercast}). Under text-exact-match scoring, a precision$\times$temperature interaction emerges at 1.3\%, consistent with \S4.1. Precision need not be controlled for standard binary evaluation at BF16/FP16 \cite{shanmugavelu2024floating}; for non-binary metrics, the text-exact-match collapse ($G = 0.007$) suggests fundamentally larger budgets are needed.

\subsection{Design-Dependent Reproducibility Budgets}

How many items does a given evaluation design need to achieve a target reliability? We translate variance components into item budgets via D-study projections.

Table~\ref{tab:dstudy} compares evaluation designs at the $G \geq 0.80$ threshold using single-model (Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct) MMLU variance components. The multi-facet design (1,296 conditions per item) achieves $G = 0.936$ at 200 items and requires only 55 items for $G \geq 0.80$. A single-condition design reaches only $G = 0.592$ at 200 items and requires 552 items---a 10.0$\times$ budget ratio stable across subset sizes (CV = 1.9\%).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{D-study reproducibility budgets under different evaluation designs (single-model, MMLU). Total evals = items $\times$ conditions.}
\label{tab:dstudy}
\scriptsize
\begin{tabular}{lcrrrc}
\toprule
Design & Cond. & $n_{G{\geq}.80}$ & Total & $G_{200}$ & $G_{116}$ \\
\midrule
Multi-facet & 1{,}296 & \textbf{55} & 71{,}280 & \textbf{.936} & --- \\
Single-cond. & 1 & 552 & 552 & .592 & .457 \\
IRT+multi & 1{,}296 & --- & 150{,}336 & --- & .895$^\ddagger$ \\
\midrule
IRT baseline & --- & 116 & 116 & --- & .457$^\dagger$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\vspace{2pt}
{\scriptsize $^\dagger$\,PSN-IRT 116 items \cite{zhou2026irt} under single-condition: $G = 0.457$. $^\ddagger$\,Same 116 items under multi-facet: $G = 0.895$.}
\end{table}

\citet{zhou2026irt}'s IRT-selected 116 items yield $G = 0.457$ under single-condition but $G = 0.895$ under multi-facet conditions, demonstrating that IRT item selection and G-theory protocol optimization are orthogonal and complementary. Forward prediction from four exp-001 models to the full eight-model design validates projection stability: MC benchmarks show $|\Delta G| \leq 0.029$ ($<$3.5\% error), while FF benchmarks show larger deviations ($|\Delta G| \leq 0.065$, $\sim$7\%) due to limited model diversity in the four-model subset---FF predictions underestimate actual $G$ (conservative), while MC predictions show mixed directions with small deviations ($|\Delta G| \leq 0.029$; Table~\ref{tab:forward_pred}).

For practitioners seeking a minimal upgrade, 3 prompts $\times$ 3 seeds ($G_{200} = 0.818$, $n_{G \geq 0.80} = 178$) is the cheapest design exceeding $G = 0.80$; a full adoption gradient is in Appendix~\ref{app:intermediate_dstudy}. Cross-model projections show budget ratios of 11--22$\times$ across benchmarks (bootstrap CIs all exceed 10$\times$; Appendix~\ref{app:dstudy_cross}), with multi-facet budgets ranging from 71 to 162 items (Table~\ref{tab:dstudy_cross}). Figure~\ref{fig:teaser} visualizes the D-study curves.

\paragraph{Projection assumptions.} Cross-model budgets increase to 93 items because model$\times$item interaction enters the relative error variance---a feature of the framework that correctly reflects multi-model evaluation requiring more items to average over model-specific difficulty profiles. The 55-item single-model and 93-item cross-model budgets answer different practical questions. Bootstrap CIs (71--162 items, spanning $\sim$1.5--3$\times$ across benchmarks) capture sampling variability but not shifts from different item pools. Forward prediction deviations of $|\Delta G| = 0.035$ translate to approximately 23\% additional items across all five benchmarks, well within this range. All projections assume the 2024--2025 model generation; we recommend periodic re-estimation as models evolve.

\subsection{Preliminary Scale Validation}

Within-family analysis (Qwen2.5 3B/7B on MMLU) shows consistent item dominance (\texttt{item\_id}: 73--75\%, $G > 0.95$). A crossed 3B$\times$7B design yields \texttt{item\_id} = 43.5\%, comparable to the 8-model reference (37.9\%), but $G_\text{item} = 0.71$ confirms two models are insufficient for reliable cross-model measurement.

\subsection{Robustness Checks}

Three independent 200-item MMLU samples yield consistent item difficulty shares (CV = 3.1\%). Treating models as fixed increases $G$ by 0.077--0.113 (mean $+0.093$), confirming random-model budgets are conservative (\S\ref{sec:model_facet}). Seven additional analyses in Appendix~\ref{app:robustness} confirm stability.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

A recommended 3-prompt $\times$ 3-seed design reduces item requirements from 552 to 178 for $G \geq 0.80$ (full multi-facet: 55 items), confirmed across all five benchmarks (bootstrap CIs exceed 10$\times$; budgets validated for 7--9B models under binary scoring). We observe suggestive but underpowered format-associated differences warranting larger benchmark samples. A minimal upgrade---two prompt variants---yields the steepest per-condition reliability gain (Appendix~\ref{app:intermediate_dstudy}); code, data, and a D-study calculator will be released upon publication.
